    "updated_at": "2017-05-11T14:15:43Z",
    "body": "My proposal would be to:\r\n\r\n- Add `OffscreenCanvasOptions` as an optional dictionary argument to `OffscreenCanvas constructor`.\r\n- This dict contains a `WebGLTexture targetTexture` that, when set, makes the OffscreenCanvas render to said texture, in a similar fashion that it does now for when an OffscreenCanvas is created from a `canvas.transferControlToOffscreen()`.\r\n\r\nAPI-interface wise is very clear and backward compatible, since implementations don't have to support this and there's polyfill that's always possible with performance penalty, assuming we are willing to gl.texImage2D every frame.\r\n\r\nAnother option would be a function `GLTexture.createOffscreenCanvas()`. This would be closer to what `canvas` usually does, but it's very different from what WebGL likes (opaque objects, without methods). Which seems to me less clear.\r\n\r\nMy current temporary implementation on Chrome pigbacks on `getContextCreationAttributes` but I think this is the worst option.",
